1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,359
On the left is what I typed, with nothing but a keyboard,

2
00:00:03,809 --> 00:00:06,046
and on the right is what a reader sees.

3
00:00:06,946 --> 00:00:09,822
The few odd characters on the left are called marks,

4
00:00:10,272 --> 00:00:12,410
and the whole idea is called markdown.

5
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:18,041
You need it because the file your mission lives in is written the same way.

6
00:00:18,941 --> 00:00:20,588
And it takes about twenty minutes.

7
00:00:21,788 --> 00:00:25,375
In a word processor, you make a heading by choosing a style,

8
00:00:25,825 --> 00:00:30,498
and the file remembers that in a way only the word processor can read.

9
00:00:31,398 --> 00:00:34,194
In markdown, you type a hash in front of the line,

10
00:00:34,644 --> 00:00:38,609
and then anything can read it: a person, a preview, or a game.

11
00:00:40,409 --> 00:00:44,228
Today you'll write one page, a story bible for your mission,

12
00:00:44,678 --> 00:00:46,540
and you'll learn six marks.

13
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:50,521
This is the folder you made and opened last time.

14
00:00:51,421 --> 00:00:55,641
I make a new file in it, and its name ends in dot M D.

15
00:00:56,541 --> 00:01:00,883
Then I type one line: a hash, a space, and my title.

16
00:01:02,683 --> 00:01:06,581
Now, control K, let go, and then V,

17
00:01:07,031 --> 00:01:08,634
and that opens the preview.

18
00:01:09,534 --> 00:01:12,261
The hash is gone, and the words have become a title.

19
00:01:13,161 --> 00:01:16,761
This preview is part of VS Code, so it needs nothing added.

20
00:01:18,561 --> 00:01:19,920
And then I save the file.

21
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:22,742
The game never reads this file.

22
00:01:23,192 --> 00:01:26,362
It's mine, and nothing I type here can break anything.

23
00:01:27,562 --> 00:01:29,328
Two hashes make a heading.

24
00:01:29,778 --> 00:01:32,322
Three would make a smaller one, inside it.

25
00:01:33,222 --> 00:01:35,110
So always count the hashes,

26
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:42,240
because in the next lecture, the number of hashes is how the game knows what's inside what.

27
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:45,769
There are two rules for headings.

28
00:01:46,669 --> 00:01:48,626
The hashes start at the left edge,

29
00:01:49,076 --> 00:01:50,811
and there's one space after them.

30
00:01:51,711 --> 00:01:53,909
Watch what happens without the space:

31
00:01:54,359 --> 00:01:55,747
it's not a heading any more.

32
00:01:56,647 --> 00:01:57,767
So I put it back.

33
00:01:58,967 --> 00:02:01,718
A paragraph can be one long line,

34
00:02:02,168 --> 00:02:04,077
and word wrap folds it for me.

35
00:02:04,977 --> 00:02:06,251
A blank line ends it.

36
00:02:08,051 --> 00:02:11,777
Now, here are two things writers do without thinking.

37
00:02:12,677 --> 00:02:16,970
The first is pressing Enter in the middle of a paragraph.

38
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:22,527
Look at the preview: it joined the halves again, so you'd never know.

39
00:02:23,427 --> 00:02:25,258
The game's Quest Log joins them too,

40
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:28,316
so in a quest's description, that's fine.

41
00:02:29,216 --> 00:02:31,877
The one place it isn't fine is a Science reading,

42
00:02:32,327 --> 00:02:33,944
and that's next lecture.

43
00:02:35,744 --> 00:02:37,698
The second is the Tab key at the start.

44
00:02:38,598 --> 00:02:40,376
Look what the preview made of that.

45
00:02:41,276 --> 00:02:45,242
In markdown, an indented line is something else altogether,

46
00:02:45,692 --> 00:02:48,434
so never start a line with Tab.

47
00:02:49,634 --> 00:02:53,313
A hyphen, a space, and a line of words make a list.

48
00:02:54,213 --> 00:02:56,250
These are the people in my story.

49
00:02:57,150 --> 00:03:01,503
A number, a period, and a space make a numbered list,

50
00:03:01,953 --> 00:03:03,708
for things that happen in order.

51
00:03:05,508 --> 00:03:07,297
Keep these three numbered lines,

52
00:03:07,747 --> 00:03:11,511
because in Lecture Eight, each one becomes a step of a quest.

53
00:03:12,411 --> 00:03:15,783
And leave a blank line above a list, and a blank line below it.

54
00:03:16,983 --> 00:03:19,011
A link is two pairs of brackets.

55
00:03:19,911 --> 00:03:22,890
Square brackets go round the words the reader sees,

56
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:25,842
and round brackets go round where it leads.

57
00:03:26,742 --> 00:03:29,239
There's nothing between them, not even a space.

58
00:03:31,039 --> 00:03:34,738
VS Code typed that closing bracket for me, so I just keep going.

59
00:03:35,638 --> 00:03:37,475
And in the preview, it's a link.

60
00:03:39,275 --> 00:03:42,637
Now here's something from the mission file, in the game first.

61
00:03:43,537 --> 00:03:46,503
This is the Science console, with the hulk scanned,

62
00:03:46,953 --> 00:03:49,954
and those words are on the hulk's scan tab.

63
00:03:50,854 --> 00:03:54,777
They come from a scan record, and this is its first line.

64
00:03:55,677 --> 00:03:59,275
It's hashes, a space, square brackets, and round brackets:

65
00:03:59,725 --> 00:04:01,484
a heading with a link in it.

66
00:04:02,384 --> 00:04:05,238
That's the first line of every record you'll ever write.

67
00:04:06,138 --> 00:04:10,703
In that file, though, the round brackets hold a short name for the game,

68
00:04:11,153 --> 00:04:12,401
and not a web address.

69
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:16,174
Two stars on each side make a word bold,

70
00:04:16,624 --> 00:04:18,783
and one star on each side makes it slanted.

71
00:04:19,683 --> 00:04:21,257
Use them in your story bible.

72
00:04:22,157 --> 00:04:24,074
But don't use them in the mission file,

73
00:04:24,524 --> 00:04:26,576
and in three minutes you'll see why.

74
00:04:27,776 --> 00:04:31,496
Everything I've typed so far is a key on my keyboard.

75
00:04:32,396 --> 00:04:34,229
A word processor isn't like that.

76
00:04:35,129 --> 00:04:38,419
I typed straight quotes here, and it made them curly.

77
00:04:38,869 --> 00:04:42,098
It did this to my hyphens, and this to my three dots.

78
00:04:42,998 --> 00:04:44,515
And it doesn't even ask me.

79
00:04:46,315 --> 00:04:48,444
The game can draw only plain characters.

80
00:04:49,344 --> 00:04:53,841
When it reads your mission, it swaps these for the plain ones, so nothing breaks,

81
00:04:54,291 --> 00:04:57,893
and you get a warning for each one, which is Lecture Six.

82
00:04:59,693 --> 00:05:03,705
But the habit starts now: write game text in VS Code.

83
00:05:04,605 --> 00:05:07,415
And if you bring words over from a word processor,

84
00:05:07,865 --> 00:05:11,299
type the quotes, the dashes and the dots again.

85
00:05:12,499 --> 00:05:14,008
Here's the file the game reads,

86
00:05:14,458 --> 00:05:16,038
and you can read most of it now.

87
00:05:16,938 --> 00:05:19,756
Hashes mean a heading, and brackets mean a link.

88
00:05:20,656 --> 00:05:23,271
But five marks in here aren't markdown's.

89
00:05:25,071 --> 00:05:27,421
This heading is the first line of a record:

90
00:05:27,871 --> 00:05:30,568
a name for people, and a key for the game.

91
00:05:31,468 --> 00:05:35,247
These three hyphens would be a line across the page in markdown.

92
00:05:35,697 --> 00:05:39,642
Here, they're a fence, with the record's facts inside.

93
00:05:41,442 --> 00:05:44,763
Now back to Science, and the hulk's scan tab.

94
00:05:45,663 --> 00:05:47,599
That sentence is one reading,

95
00:05:48,049 --> 00:05:52,511
and a percent sign in the file starts each reading the hulk can give.

96
00:05:53,411 --> 00:05:55,414
This record has two of them,

97
00:05:55,864 --> 00:05:59,112
and Science picks one each time the hulk is scanned.

98
00:06:00,912 --> 00:06:04,458
Two slashes are a note to myself, which the game skips.

99
00:06:05,357 --> 00:06:08,857
And there's one you haven't got yet, double square brackets,

100
00:06:09,307 --> 00:06:11,132
which is a link to another record.

101
00:06:12,932 --> 00:06:17,623
So this file is shaped like markdown, and it isn't markdown.

102
00:06:18,523 --> 00:06:22,104
The preview I opened for my story bible doesn't open for it.

103
00:06:22,554 --> 00:06:24,236
The game is what reads it.

104
00:06:25,436 --> 00:06:27,373
So what does the game do with marks?

105
00:06:28,273 --> 00:06:30,670
I'll give this quest twelve lines:

106
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:34,984
a heading, a paragraph typed on two lines, a list,

107
00:06:35,434 --> 00:06:37,599
a small table, and a link,

108
00:06:38,049 --> 00:06:39,408
and then I save the file.

109
00:06:41,208 --> 00:06:45,102
I start the game the way we did last time, and I don't fly anywhere.

110
00:06:46,002 --> 00:06:49,522
On Helm, it's the handheld, then Quests,

111
00:06:49,972 --> 00:06:54,019
and in the Quest Log, I select Find the Derelict.

112
00:06:54,919 --> 00:06:58,238
The pane beside the list shows what the crew is told about it.

113
00:07:00,038 --> 00:07:02,460
The heading is drawn as a heading.

114
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,345
My two lines are one paragraph,

115
00:07:05,795 --> 00:07:07,708
just as the preview joined them.

116
00:07:08,608 --> 00:07:11,222
But the bold word is still wearing its stars.

117
00:07:12,122 --> 00:07:13,774
The list is a list,

118
00:07:14,224 --> 00:07:16,326
and the table is a table.

119
00:07:17,226 --> 00:07:21,622
And the link shows every bracket and the whole address, with nothing to click.

120
00:07:23,422 --> 00:07:24,920
Look at the spacing, too.

121
00:07:25,369 --> 00:07:28,595
Where I left a blank line, the game left a gap.

122
00:07:29,495 --> 00:07:31,692
So a description is a page,

123
00:07:32,142 --> 00:07:35,007
and you can give the crew a lot more than one sentence.

124
00:07:36,807 --> 00:07:40,291
Here are the rules for the mission file, and they're on the page.

125
00:07:41,191 --> 00:07:43,269
Write a description as a page,

126
00:07:43,719 --> 00:07:47,550
with paragraphs, and a heading, a list or a table where they help.

127
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:51,377
Leave out stars, backticks and links.

128
00:07:52,277 --> 00:07:54,209
And a Science reading is one line,

129
00:07:54,659 --> 00:07:57,569
because every line is a reading of its own.

130
00:07:59,369 --> 00:08:02,270
Then I close the game, and I put my line back,

131
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:05,760
because the next lecture starts from the file as it was.

132
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:09,242
Now write the story bible for your own mission.

133
00:08:10,142 --> 00:08:13,032
Give it a title, and the story in two sentences.

134
00:08:13,932 --> 00:08:18,878
Then three headings: premise, people, and what happens.

135
00:08:19,778 --> 00:08:24,142
Add three people, three things that happen, and one link.

136
00:08:25,942 --> 00:08:29,321
Keep it, because the people become characters in Class Two,

137
00:08:29,771 --> 00:08:33,230
and the three things that happen become your first quest.

138
00:08:34,130 --> 00:08:38,285
Next time, we take one record of the mission file apart, line by line.
